From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 14:04:34 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: aop: Take the T6040 firmware segments from
 memory-region

On the T6040 the driver found the firmware's external code and log
segments by looking for /reserved-memory nodes named aop-etext and
aop-oslog, and mapped them with iommu_map() into the device's DMA API
domain, without its IOVA allocator knowing about them.

Take them from the AOP's memory-region instead, which is how the
bootloader describes the firmware of the other Apple coprocessors. Each
region's iommu-addresses gives the IOVA the firmware uses. The IOMMU core
already maps such regions in the default domain when the device is
added, one to one for the log and translated for the external code, and
keeps their IOVAs out of the allocator, so the driver no longer maps or
unmaps anything itself.

Before the firmware is started, check that every page of every region is
mapped where the DT says, that one region sits at EC0p and that any
other one is mapped at its physical address, and refuse to start it
otherwise. The check now runs before anything is set up, so a missing
region no longer ends in a failed shutdown of an AOP that never ran.

The other AOP generations are unchanged.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
(cherry picked from commit b10c2f9d72939d565b83102ce4ddf542aa7c55f7)
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
